\[ \text{proj}_{\begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}} \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \frac{\begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}}{\begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}} \begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \frac{2*4 + 1*1}{4*4 + 1*1} \begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \frac{9}{17} \begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{36}{17} \\ \frac{9}{17} \end{pmatrix}. \]
To find the point on the line that corresponds to this projection, we use the fact that the parameter $t$ of the line corresponding to this point is the same as the scalar projection we just found, but scaled by the direction vector. Thus, the point on the line is $\left( 4 \cdot \frac{9}{17} + 2, \frac{9}{17} + 2 \right) = \left( \frac{36}{17} + \frac{34}{17}, \frac{9}{17} + \frac{34}{17} \right) = \left( \frac{70}{17}, \frac{43}{17} \right)$. The vector from the origin to this point is $\begin{pmatrix} \frac{70}{17} \\ \frac{43}{17} \end{pmatrix}$, which simplifies to $\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \end{pmatrix}$. The correct answer is:
